arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:2:name: megaplan-epic
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:3:description: Run an epic — a chain of sprint-sized megaplans driven sequentially via `megaplan chain`. Use when the work is bigger than ~2 weeks and needs to be decomposed into multiple plans with state, ordering, and failure semantics handled by the harness.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:6:# Megaplan Epic
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:8:An **epic** is work too big for a single megaplan, decomposed into an ordered chain of sprint-sized megaplans driven sequentially by `megaplan chain`. Each milestone in the chain is a full megaplan run (its own brief, plan, critique, execute, review); the chain handles ordering, state persistence, branch/PR lifecycle, and failure semantics.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:10:If your work fits in one megaplan, you don't need this skill — read **megaplan-prep** and run a single sprint. Reach for the epic flow when the answer to "size each megaplan to ~2 weeks of work" is "this doesn't fit."
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:16:- **Multiple major architectural decisions** that each deserve their own brief + critique pass — pretending it's one sprint flattens decisions that need separate deliberation.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:22:- **Exploration / discovery work** where you don't yet know the milestone breakdown. Run a single megaplan first to scope; then write the chain spec.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:23:- **Truly independent sprints.** If sprints don't depend on each other, just run them sequentially as plain `megaplan init` calls — a chain spec is overhead with no benefit.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:24:- **Anything where the milestone breakdown isn't pre-decided.** Chains run unattended through the declared spec; if you'd want to look at milestone 1's output and decide what milestone 2 should be, just run them as separate megaplans.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:26:## Terminology — epic vs chain vs `megaplan epic`
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:30:- **Epic** (this skill) — the *concept*: multi-sprint work decomposed into a chain of megaplans.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:31:- **`megaplan chain`** — the *imperative verb* that drives the flow. This is what you actually run.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:32:- **`megaplan epic`** — a *data-admin verb* for snapshot / migrate / export of the editorial epic record. Not the orchestration entry point. Don't confuse the two.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:36:A chain spec is a YAML file declaring the base branch, an optional seed plan, and an ordered list of milestones. Each milestone has its own rubric knobs (profile, robustness, depth, vendor, prep/feedback flags).
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:38:Store durable epic artifacts under `.megaplan/initiatives/<epic-slug>/`: put the executable `chain.yaml` at the initiative root and keep milestone brief files under `briefs/`. Single-plan idea briefs live at `.megaplan/initiatives/<slug>/briefs/<slug>.md`. `.megaplan/plans/` remains generated runtime state; `.megaplan/initiatives/` is the committed source material that creates runs.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:47:# Optional: a pre-existing plan whose output seeds the first milestone's repo state.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:49:  plan: scoping-from-docs-20260415-0217
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:53:    idea: .megaplan/initiatives/artifact-store/briefs/m1-schema.md
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:60:    idea: .megaplan/initiatives/artifact-store/briefs/m2-storage.md
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:66:    idea: .megaplan/initiatives/artifact-store/briefs/m3-api.md
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:73:    idea: .megaplan/initiatives/artifact-store/briefs/m4-docs.md
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:74:    profile: directed                # tier 2 — docs benefit from a smart plan, cheap to execute
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:80:merge_policy: auto                   # default: auto. Use for unattended/cloud epics so clean milestone PRs merge and the chain advances; use review/manual only when the user explicitly wants a human PR gate after every milestone.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:89:### North Star requirement
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:91:Epics require a top-level North Star by default. Put `NORTHSTAR.md` beside `chain.yaml` and declare it with `anchors.north_star: NORTHSTAR.md`. The North Star is the durable destination for the whole chain; milestone briefs narrow local scope, and milestone anchors may extend the destination for a slice, but they do not replace the top-level epic anchor.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:96:megaplan chain start --spec .megaplan/initiatives/my-epic/chain.yaml \
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:109:Do not rely on a colocated `NORTHSTAR.md` file without declaring it. Anchors are explicit and are snapshotted into each milestone plan at initialization.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:116:| `idea` | yes | Path to the brief markdown file. Same as `megaplan init <idea>`. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:117:| `profile` | no | `solo` / `directed` / `partnered` / `premium` / `apex`. See megaplan-prep. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:124:| `anchors` | no | Currently supports `north_star: <path>`. Milestone anchors extend the top-level epic North Star for that milestone. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:140:Use `launch_preconditions` when a chain must not start until a prerequisite file, source path, or chain completion proof exists. For ordinary dependent chains, `kind: chain_completed` verifies the prerequisite chain state, current `chain.yaml` hash, completed milestone labels, plan names, and merged PR evidence for review-merge chains.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:142:For review-gated launch evidence, use an artifact check with `kind: review_log_clean` instead of only checking that a review log contains a marker string. It fails launch if the log contains an explicit `BLOCK` verdict or a `PASS WITH EDIT` section without an applied-edits note:
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:146:  - name: review log has no unaddressed blockers
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:147:    path: docs/arnold/my-review-log.md
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:149:      kind: review_log_clean
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:158:    chain: .megaplan/initiatives/platform-substrate/chain.yaml
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:166:megaplan chain manifest \
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:167:  --spec .megaplan/initiatives/platform-substrate/chain.yaml \
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:168:  --proof-map .megaplan/initiatives/platform-substrate/proof-map.json
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:180:`require_manifest: true` is optional harness functionality, but use it when a downstream chain could waste work or drift if a prerequisite was only nominally complete. It blocks launch when the prerequisite manifest is missing, stale, has no proof artifacts, or no longer matches the current chain spec, North Star, milestone briefs, state records, PR metadata when applicable, or proof file hashes.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:182:## Per-milestone rubric — same dials as megaplan-prep
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:184:Each milestone is a full megaplan. The three dials (`profile` / `robustness` / `depth`) apply per-milestone — see **megaplan-prep** for how to pick them. **Milestones in the same chain can be different tiers.** A typical epic has one or two high-stakes milestones at `premium` or `apex` and several mechanical milestones at `partnered` or `directed`.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:186:The shorthand from megaplan-prep works for chain-spec notes: a milestone block annotated `# partnered//high +prep` in your chain.yaml comments tells the reader the intent at a glance.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:192:megaplan chain start --spec /path/to/chain.yaml
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:195:megaplan chain start --spec /path/to/chain.yaml \
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:197:  --missing-anchor-ack "Reason this epic does not need a North Star."
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:200:megaplan chain start --spec /path/to/chain.yaml --one
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:203:megaplan chain status --spec /path/to/chain.yaml
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:214:Progress is persisted under `.megaplan/plans/.chains/<spec-stem>-<digest>.json`. The digest is computed from the resolved spec path, so the same spec resumes deterministically. To resume after an interruption, just re-run `megaplan chain start --spec <same path>` — the driver reads the state file, skips completed milestones, and picks up at the current one.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:219:- Failed milestones can be re-attempted by deleting the milestone's plan directory and re-running — the chain will redrive that milestone.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:224:For chains that need to outlive your terminal session, run them inside `megaplan cloud` with `mode: chain` in `cloud.yaml`. The container drives the chain unattended; you observe via `megaplan cloud status` / `cloud logs` / `cloud attach`. See `docs/cloud.md` for the cloud reference.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:226:Cloud epics should normally keep `merge_policy: auto` and `driver.auto_approve: true`. `merge_policy: review`/`manual` intentionally parks the chain at `awaiting_pr_merge` after every milestone PR; do not use it for unattended runs unless the user asked for human PR review gates.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:228:When supervising a long cloud chain, follow the cadence in the main megaplan skill: check after launch, again after 10-15 min, then hourly.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:234:**1. Decompose into milestones.** Create an initiative directory under `.megaplan/initiatives/` and write one idea file per milestone under its `briefs/` directory, each sized to ~1 week:
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:237:.megaplan/initiatives/artifact-store/briefs/m1-schema.md  # schema + invariants
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:238:.megaplan/initiatives/artifact-store/briefs/m2-storage.md # storage layer against the schema
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:239:.megaplan/initiatives/artifact-store/briefs/m3-api.md     # public API over storage
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:240:.megaplan/initiatives/artifact-store/briefs/m4-docs.md    # docs + migration guide
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:241:.megaplan/initiatives/artifact-store/NORTHSTAR.md         # durable end-state intent
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:242:.megaplan/initiatives/artifact-store/chain.yaml           # chain spec
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:245:Each idea file is a full brief (see the "What goes in the brief" section of megaplan-prep) — outcome, scope, locked decisions, open questions, constraints, done criteria, touchpoints, anti-scope. Briefs are locked at init; later edits are not re-read.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:252:- m4 docs → `directed` (docs benefit from a smart plan; execution is cheap)
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:257:megaplan chain start --spec .megaplan/initiatives/artifact-store/chain.yaml
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:260:For a long unattended run, do this inside `megaplan cloud` so it survives the terminal.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:265:megaplan chain status --spec .megaplan/initiatives/artifact-store/chain.yaml
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:268:Shows current milestone index, current plan name, last state, completed milestones, and PR state if applicable.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:270:**5. If a milestone fails or escalates**, the chain halts (with default `stop_chain`). Investigate the failing plan via `megaplan status --plan <name>` and `megaplan audit --plan <name>`. Once you've fixed the brief or escalated the rubric (via `megaplan override set-profile` etc.), re-run `megaplan chain start --spec` and the chain resumes.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/epic_skill.md:275:- **Don't reach for a chain when you don't know the breakdown yet.** Run a scoping megaplan first; let it produce the milestone list; then write the chain spec.
