arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:2:name: megaplan-prep
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:3:description: Set up a megaplan run before invoking it — size the work, write the brief, score overall plan difficulty, and choose robustness/depth. Consult before every `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan init`.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:6:# Megaplan Setup
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:12:| 1 | How model-quality-sensitive is the planning loop? | **Overall plan difficulty** | model quality for plan/revise/finalize | `--profile partnered-3|partnered-4|partnered-5` |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:16:**Always run a megaplan, even for tiny work.** The harness captures the brief, plan, execution, and outcome — that record is worth the few seconds of overhead. `bare` is the floor; there is no "skip megaplan" option.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:18:**Run megaplan inside a subagent by default**, off the main thread — keeping the orchestrating conversation thin while the harness handles its own multi-phase chatter. On-thread is the exception, reserved for when you want to watch each phase live. The subagent is the venue; megaplan is still the harness — never skip megaplan in favor of "just doing it in a subagent."
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:24:**Defaults to keep in mind: default to `--profile partnered-5` unless the user instructs otherwise.** Prefer maximum planning/critique quality even when the work looks small; only step down to `partnered-4` or `partnered-3` when the user asks for a lighter run or you can name a concrete reason the work is genuinely trivial (decisions already locked, single localized change, failure trivially detected). Still score overall plan difficulty 1–5 for the record and to justify any downward deviation. Keep `--vendor` at your config default, `--robustness full`, and `--depth` unset unless you can name the specific reason to change them. Built-in profiles live in `megaplan/profiles/`; per-user (`~/.config/megaplan/profiles.toml`) and per-project (`<project>/.megaplan/profiles.toml`) TOML overrides win over them.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:30:Two decisions come before the three dials: **how many megaplans you need**, and **what each brief covers**. Get these wrong and the dials can't save the run.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:32:### Size each megaplan to ~2 weeks of work
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:34:A megaplan should fit roughly **two weeks of human work** — the time a skilled engineer would take to plan, build, and review the same scope. Wall-clock for the harness itself is unrelated; this is about scope, not duration.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:36:If the work is bigger, **split it into an epic** — a chain of sprint-sized megaplans driven sequentially by `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan chain`. Each sprint in the chain gets its own brief, its own overall plan difficulty score, its own profile, and its own retrospective. See the **megaplan-epic** skill for spec format, per-milestone rubric, and end-to-end usage. Cramming a month of work into one plan means the brief drifts, the critique loses focus, and the review can't hold the whole shape in one pass.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:38:For epics, migrations, public contract changes, or cross-cutting refactors, write a North Star before kickoff. The North Star is the durable end-state intent that every milestone should preserve; milestone briefs should narrow local scope without redefining that destination. Epics require a top-level `anchors.north_star` by default; single-plan North Stars remain optional.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:48:For high-confidence epic handoffs, use the chain completion manifest feature instead of relying only on prose or chain state. A downstream `chain.yaml` can require a prerequisite chain to be complete with `kind: chain_completed` plus `require_manifest: true`; the prerequisite chain then emits `completion-manifest.json` via `megaplan chain manifest --spec ... --proof-map ...`. The manifest hashes the chain spec, North Star when declared, milestone briefs, and explicit proof artifacts; it also records completed milestone labels, plan names, and review-merge PR metadata when applicable. Downstream launch then rejects missing, stale, or mismatched manifest evidence.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:50:**But: one profile per sprint.** Within a sprint, score the overall planning difficulty for the sprint as a whole. Operational simplicity beats the savings from splitting by difficulty alone. Only split when lower-difficulty work is *substantial* (multiple days) **and** independent. Structure the plan so easier work lives in easier sprints, not interleaved inside harder ones.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:58:**Store durable briefs in `.megaplan/initiatives/<slug>/`.** Single-plan ideas live at `.megaplan/initiatives/<slug>/briefs/<slug>.md`. Epics live at `.megaplan/initiatives/<epic-slug>/chain.yaml` with milestone briefs under `briefs/`. `.megaplan/plans/` is generated run state; `.megaplan/initiatives/` is the committed input material you hand to `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan init`, `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan chain start`, or `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan cloud chain`. Use `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan initiative new` for new work; `brief new` / `brief epic` are compatibility helpers around the same canonical layout.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:63:python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan initiative new <slug> \
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:68:This creates `.megaplan/initiatives/<slug>/README.md`, `NORTHSTAR.md`, `briefs/<milestone>.md`, `chain.yaml`, and an initiative-local `cloud.yaml`. It is cloud-ready, but deliberately not silently runnable with nonsense defaults: generated placeholders such as `TODO_INITIATIVE_DESCRIPTION`, `TODO_NORTH_STAR_*`, `TODO_REPO_URL`, and `TODO_SSH_HOST` make `cloud preflight` / `cloud chain` fail until the operator edits them or passes the explicit override `--allow-template-placeholders`.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:73:python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan initiative new code-smell-first-aid \
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:83:For single plans, pass `--north-star path/to/NORTHSTAR.md` at `init` when the local brief needs durable alignment context. This is optional, but strongly recommended for drift-sensitive standalone plans: migrations, public contracts, cross-cutting refactors, multi-agent handoffs, or any task where local success criteria could accidentally narrow the intended destination. For epics, keep `NORTHSTAR.md` beside `chain.yaml` and declare it as `anchors.north_star`; `brief epic` scaffolds this by default and chain runs require it unless explicitly opted out. Anchor files are snapshotted into plan state at initialization, so edit the source before starting the run.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:87:1. **Outcome** — what's being delivered, in one or two sentences. The thing a reviewer would check.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:89:3. **Locked decisions** — architecture, interfaces, libraries, patterns already chosen. Naming them stops the planner from relitigating.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:90:4. **Open questions** — things you don't know yet that the planner needs to resolve. Naming them stops the planner from quietly inventing answers.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:94:8. **Anti-scope** — explicit "don't touch X" or "don't refactor Y" so the planner doesn't drift into bonus work.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:96:A brief missing #3 or #4 surfaces those gaps as critique flags — better to write them down up front than have the harness rediscover them mid-run.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:102:> **"How model-quality-sensitive is the planning/revision/adjudication loop?"**
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:104:Score the whole plan's residual difficulty from **1 to 5** before choosing the profile. This is not the same as per-task execution complexity: it decides how strong the planning/revision/adjudication loop should be for the run as a whole. Task execution still gets routed later by `finalize` through `tier_models.execute`.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:106:The score is intentionally more granular than the profile map: scores `1`, `2`, and `3` all use `partnered-3`. The extra resolution is for auditability and repeatability, not fake precision. Only move to `partnered-4` or `partnered-5` when the plan itself is model-quality-sensitive.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:113:| `4` | `partnered-4` | Hard planning or decomposition: unfamiliar code, cross-system behavior, subtle ordering, import/package topology, or a difficult task-difficulty adjudication problem. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:114:| `5` | `partnered-5` | Highest-stakes or hardest plans: architecture pivots, production data, security, public contracts, migrations, or failures that could pass tests while causing non-local damage. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:120:- Use `5` when a bad plan could still pass local tests while damaging a contract, invariant, migration path, data model, security boundary, or downstream architecture.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:121:- Score the highest plausible planning failure, not the scariest noun. Production data, auth, or schemas do not automatically mean `5` if the actual change is local and well-proven.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:128:- Need more deliberation from the same planner -> higher `--depth`.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:134:Overall plan difficulty: N/5; selected profile: partnered-3|partnered-4|partnered-5; because: <one sentence naming the planning failure being guarded against>.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:143:The `--robustness` flag. Picks how many phases run and how many critique passes happen. The five levels form a coherent process-completeness scale: **bare → light → full → thorough → extreme**.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:147:| `bare` | plan → finalize → execute (no prep, no critique, no gate, no review) | **The floor — use this when nothing heavier earns its cost.** Single-file fixes, mechanical changes, tasks you'd otherwise do inline. The 3-phase run captures what you did and why, even when critique would be a no-op. Always preferable to skipping the harness. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:148:| `light` | plan → critique → revise → finalize → execute (no prep, no gate, no review) | Small/scoped, well-known feature, low blast radius — but you want **one** sense-check pass on the plan before committing. ~5 phases instead of 8. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:149:| `full` *(default)* | prep → plan → critique → gate → revise → finalize → execute → review; up to 6 critique lenses | Cross-cutting, unfamiliar code, ambiguous brief. **This is almost always perfect for everything.** |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:150:| `thorough` | Same shape as `full`, up to 9 critique lenses + parallel critique | Security, data migration, public API contract — anything where a regression = production incident. **Extremely rare.** You should be able to name the specific stakes that warrant it. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:151:| `extreme` | `thorough` + parallel review | Both deep critique *and* concurrent review matter. **Vanishingly rare.** Only when the user specifically asks for it. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:155:The "critique lenses" counts above are the **static** lens pools used when adaptive critique is **off**. When adaptive critique is **on**, the evaluator selects which lenses fire from the same 9-lens catalog per iteration — the robustness dial no longer fixes a count; the evaluator does (see [`docs/critique.md`](critique.md)). Robustness still governs the surrounding workflow shape (whether `gate`/`review` run, whether prep/parallel critique are forced).
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:167:| `low` planner / `low` critic | **The default.** The pattern is mechanical, intuition is enough, the codebase is well-known. A lot of work lands here even at tier 3 — premium models at `low` thinking are still substantially smarter than DeepSeek, so the upgrade isn't free but doesn't need to be expensive either. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:168:| `medium` planner / `low` critic | Brief is clear but the work has real judgment calls. The plan needs deliberation beyond intuition; the critic still doesn't. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:169:| `high` planner / `low` critic | Brief is long OR codebase is unfamiliar. The planner needs substantial repo-reading and structural reasoning. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:170:| `xhigh` / `max` planner only | Genuinely novel architectural decision. Use sparingly — most "hard" plans don't actually need this. |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:174:**The asymmetry principle:** author phases (plan, revise) can scale all the way up to `max` when the work demands deliberation; sense-check phases (critique, gate, review) plateau at `low` regardless of stakes. A `claude:high` planner + `claude:low` critic is the right shape when the plan needs real thinking — not `claude:medium` everywhere.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:176:Default to `low`; only spend on depth when you can name the specific reason the planner needs to deliberate. "Just in case" doesn't earn the cost.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:182:**If a run is struggling, escalate mid-flight rather than letting it grind.** Common signals: the plan keeps missing concerns critique surfaces; revise doesn't resolve the critique's flags; the executor produces work review can't accept; iteration cycles through the same defects without converging. Don't sit through a degenerate run — one wasted phase costs much less than restarting the sprint.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:184:- `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan override set-profile --profile NAME --plan ID` — swap profile mid-run. Started on `partnered-3`, hit something gnarlier, escalate to `partnered-4` or `partnered-5` for the remainder.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:185:- `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan override set-robustness --robustness LEVEL --plan ID` — same for the planning-complexity dial.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:186:- `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan override replan --plan ID` — back up to planning and redo with whatever models / robustness are now active.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:187:- `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan override add-note --plan ID --note "..."` — inject guidance into an active plan without restarting any phase. Read by every subsequent phase. The brief is snapshotted at `init`; later edits to the idea-file are NOT re-read, so this is the verb for "I missed something." **`python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan feedback` is end-of-run rating, not in-flight guidance** — common confusion.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:199:> **"Does the planner need to do explicit research before it can commit to a plan?"**
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:201:`prep` is a visible research phase that runs *before* `plan` — the planner explicitly reads external docs, surveys an unfamiliar library, maps an API surface, or disambiguates a vague brief. Enable with `--with-prep`.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:205:- **External APIs whose semantics aren't already known** — the planner has to read API docs before deciding what calls to make.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:206:- **Unfamiliar libraries or frameworks** — codebase patterns aren't enough; the planner needs to survey the library's API surface first.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:208:- **Ambiguous or under-specified requirements** — the planner needs a budget to disambiguate explicitly instead of interleaving with planning.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:213:**Steering prep with `--prep-direction`.** When prep runs (either via `--with-prep` or because robustness is `thorough`/`extreme`), you can hand it explicit guidance about *what* to explore: `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan init … --prep-direction "focus on the worker shutdown path; ignore CLI plumbing"`. It's shown to the prep worker as a distinct "User direction for prep" section — steering, not a replacement for the task. Use it when prep would otherwise wander (broad codebase, multiple plausible entry points) or when you want it to skip the obvious file and trace a specific call chain. You can also set or replace it after init with `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan prep --direction "…"` before the phase runs, and chain milestones accept `prep_direction:` per milestone. Has no effect if prep is skipped.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:219:`--with-feedback` adds a `feedback` step between `review` and `done` that scaffolds `feedback.md` (a per-stage ratings template) and then completes the plan. Enable with `--with-feedback`.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:238:| `partnered-3` | Overall plan difficulty 1–3, defaults throughout |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:239:| `partnered-4//high` | Overall plan difficulty 4, high depth, default robustness |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:240:| `partnered-3//high @codex +prep` | Overall plan difficulty 1–3, high depth, codex vendor, with prep phase |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:241:| `partnered-5/thorough/high` | Overall plan difficulty 5, thorough, high depth |
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:245:The shorthand is for recording (sprint notes, brief headers, commit messages), not for the CLI. The actual invocation is still `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan init --profile … --robustness … --depth …` — see "Running it" below.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:255:1. **`--profile`** — `partnered-3`, `partnered-4`, or `partnered-5`, chosen from the overall plan difficulty score.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:257:3. **`--depth low|medium|high|xhigh|max`** — rewrites the effort suffix on author-side claude/codex slots (plan, revise, loop_plan, tiebreaker_*) at the resolved vendor. Critic + mechanical phases plateau at their existing depth (the asymmetry principle). Defaults to whatever the profile sets (usually `:low`). Honored on vendor-locked profiles. Codex caps at `high`; Claude adds `xhigh` and `max`.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:261:- **`--vendor claude|codex`** — vendor override where the selected profile exposes premium vendor slots. Defaults to `[defaults].vendor` in `~/.config/megaplan/config.toml` (or `codex` if unset).
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:262:- **`--critic cross`** — overrides the critique+review pair to the other premium vendor relative to `--vendor`, when supported by the selected profile.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:265:- **`--prep-direction "…"`** — steering text shown to the prep worker (when prep runs) as a "User direction for prep" section. Points prep at specific files / subsystems / questions to explore. Can also be set or replaced later with `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan prep --direction "…"` before the phase runs. No-op if prep is skipped. See "Optional phases" above.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:266:- **`--with-feedback`** — force the `feedback` phase into the workflow regardless of `--robustness`. Scaffolds `feedback.md` (a per-stage ratings template) between `review` and `done`, then completes the plan non-interactively. Off by default. See "Optional phases" above.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:270:**`--phase-model phase=spec`**, repeatable. For when `--depth` is too coarse — e.g. bump just `critique` without touching the rest. Most runs don't need it.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:272:For an in-flight plan, `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan override set-model --phase PHASE --model MODEL`
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:279:string arrays (e.g., `plan = ["claude", "codex"]`) to declare an ordered
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:281:megaplan skill. TOML arrays are the only v1 entry point for fallback chains;
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:286:Important: `--phase-model critique=...` and `override set-model --phase critique`
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:287:pin the critique **phase/orchestrator**. They do not by themselves pin the
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:288:per-lens critics chosen by adaptive critique. In the normal adaptive path, a
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:289:premium evaluator/director may run first and then dispatch the selected critique
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:290:lenses to cheaper DeepSeek/Kimi-style workers; seeing `critique_evaluator` on a
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:291:premium model followed by `critique` on Hermes/DeepSeek is expected. Only pin
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:295:### The critique == review invariant
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:297:The model that critiques the plan also reviews the executed work — same mind pre-execution and post-execution. Wiring them to the same non-author model gives you one coherent second mind across both checkpoints and keeps the author's blindspots out of the sense-check loop.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:299:`--critic` bundles the two phases in one flag and preserves the invariant. Bare `--phase-model` does not — if you override critique with `--phase-model`, override review the same way, or use `--critic` instead.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:301:**Exception — `partnered-*`:** critique may run on cheap DeepSeek under the premium *critique-evaluator's* direction (adaptive critique is on by default), while review stays premium. The premium **director** — not a strict same-model invariant — is what keeps the critique phase honest: the evaluator picks the lenses the cheap critic runs and rejects weak findings, so you get premium-grade critique judgment without paying for a premium critic model on every lens.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:305:`python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan init --in-worktree NAME` spins up a dedicated git worktree at `~/Documents/.megaplan-worktrees/<NAME>/` on a new branch, so each sprint lives in its own checkout. Use it for multi-PR migrations, or when concurrent work on `main` shouldn't be disturbed. Substitutes for `--project-dir`.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:312:Skip `--in-worktree` for small one-shot plans, bakeoff runs (orchestrator manages its own worktrees), or when extending an existing worktree (use `--project-dir <path>` instead).
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:317:> `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan init <brief> --profile partnered-3`
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:320:> `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan init <brief> --profile partnered-4 --depth high`
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:323:> `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan init <brief> --profile partnered-3 --with-prep`
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:326:> `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan init <brief> --profile partnered-5 --robustness thorough --depth high`
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:329:> `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan init <brief> --profile partnered-5 --robustness thorough --depth high`
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:331:> *"Tier 3, brief is clear, but I want the critic specifically to deliberate more — leave the planner alone."*
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:332:> `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan init <brief> --profile partnered-3 --phase-model critique=claude:medium --phase-model review=claude:medium` *(surgical: bumps just the critic+review pair — preserving the critique==review invariant — and leaves plan/revise at the profile's default. `--depth` can't express this because it's by-phase-name, not by-author-vs-critic.)*
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:338:The `--vendor` flag honors a per-user config default. Write `~/.config/megaplan/config.toml`:
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:355:## Watching and diagnosing a running plan
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:357:This skill covers profile/robustness/depth selection *before* a run. Once a plan is in flight, switch to the **`megaplan-observe`** skill — same author, complementary focus:
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:359:- **Pull-mode observation**: `python -m arnold_pipelines.megaplan introspect` / `trace` / `doctor` for on-demand inspection, blockage diagnosis, drift detection. Read it before reaching for `override` so you don't guess at an `invalid_transition`.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:360:- **Push-mode observation**: `watcher.sh` (bundled in the same skill) is a bash polling loop that streams phase-transition notifications. Wire it through Claude Code's `Monitor` tool to get told when phases start/end, when cost climbs, and when the plan reaches a terminal state — no manual polling.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/data/prep_skill.md:362:When something looks wrong during a run (cost spiking, phase not advancing, iteration counter stuck), `megaplan-observe` is the next stop, not `--max-cost-usd`. The cost-cap and rework-cap flags exist for narrow recovery cases; they are not a default. Trust the defaults; intervene with `override` + tests if a phase fixates.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:3:description: Three-round adversarial critique of epic drafts (high / mid / low abstraction) with revision after each round. Produces a chain-ready revised epic.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:8:A three-round pipeline for rigorous epic critique and revision. Fifteen
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:9:independent critics across three abstraction levels (high, mid, low) review
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:13:epic is ready for `megaplan chain` decomposition into milestone briefs.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:19:- You have a draft epic document and want adversarial review before committing to chain planning.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:21:- You want a structured, artifact-oriented critique process rather than ad-hoc human review.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:22:- You need a readiness assessment before running `megaplan chain`.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:26:- Prose or document review (use `writing-panel-strict` for that).
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:27:- Sprint-level critique (Epic Blitz is for epic-level documents).
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:31:- `draft` (file, required): Path to the epic markdown draft to review.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:37:megaplan run epic-blitz path/to/epic.md
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:40:megaplan run epic-blitz --inputs draft=path/to/epic.md
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:43:megaplan run epic-blitz path/to/epic.md --profile @epic-blitz:standard
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:48:Epic Blitz runs three critique-and-revision rounds, each at a different
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:53:1. **High Panel** (parallel) — five critics review the draft:
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:55:   - `conceptual_fit` — does this belong in megaplan's model?
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:63:3. **Mid Panel** (parallel) — five critics review the revised epic:
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:73:5. **Low Panel** (parallel) — five critics review the revised epic:
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:83:After a successful run, the plan directory contains:
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:86:<plan_dir>/
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/SKILL.md:122:- Epic Blitz v1 is fully non-interactive. All 15 critics and 3 revisers run without human gates.
arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipelines/epic-blitz/prompts/high/epic_decomposition.md:3:You are working from the **latest revised epic artifact** (not the original draft). The artifact you receive already incorporates revisions from prior critique rounds.
