 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Megaplan Native Python Representation Report
     2	
     3	## 1. Executive summary
     4	
     5	In this report, "native" means more than "the workflow is authored in Python." It means the workflow's product semantics are visible in the Python control flow itself: loops are loops, gates are branches, tiebreakers are subworkflows, review rework is an explicit cycle, human intervention is a suspension point, and task execution fanout is not hidden behind a single opaque handler call.
     6	
     7	The current Megaplan planning workflow is Python-authored, but it is still largely an explicit-node manifest graph with handler references. The top-level file `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/workflows/planning.py` defines 13 steps and a route table, but the real behavior of the product is encoded inside handlers in `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/handlers/`, execution code in `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/execute/`, and auto-drive/runtime helpers.
     8	
     9	The main finding: a truly native Megaplan pipeline would look like a normal Python program with durable phase calls. Its top-level structure would include:
    10	
    11	- a prep clarification gate;
    12	- adaptive critique evaluation with retry;
    13	- parallel critique lenses with fan-in;
    14	- a bounded critique/gate/revise loop with severity-aware termination;
    15	- a tiebreaker subworkflow with researcher/challenger branches and a human decision;
    16	- finalize fallback routes;
    17	- dependency-aware execution over runtime task batches;
    18	- execute/review/rework loops;
    19	- human override and force-proceed routes;
    20	- explicit timeout, retry, escalation, model-routing, and suspension policies.
    21	
    22	The repo already has many of the ingredients. Existing native pipeline infrastructure includes `@pipeline`, `@phase`, `@decision`, fixed parallel blocks, native IR compilation, graph projection, bounded loops, suspension routes, retry policy slots, control transition slots, and subpipeline references. The largest missing pieces for Megaplan are runtime-list iteration, dynamic parallel map, source-level retry/timeout/model-routing policy, first-class break/continue or typed loop outcomes, and a top-level way to describe auto-drive/event transitions without forcing handlers to mutate state as a side effect.
    23	
    24	This report was produced with five DeepSeek subagents launched through a patched temporary copy of the Hermes launcher. The unmodified launcher/fan script failed against this worktree because it expected the older `arnold.pipelines.megaplan` import path; the temporary launcher changed those imports to the current `arnold.agent` and `arnold_pipelines.megaplan.runtime` modules. The research outputs were then spot-checked against source files before synthesis.
    25	
    26	## 2. Current state: explicit-node DSL plus handlers
    27	
    28	The current Megaplan source authority is split across several files. The
    29	product-readable authored source is
    30	`arnold_pipelines/megaplan/workflows/workflow.py`; the package-facing builder is
    31	`arnold_pipelines/megaplan/workflows/planning.py`, which lowers/canonicalizes
    32	that source into the explicit-node `arnold.workflow.dsl.Pipeline` shape consumed
    33	by manifest/runtime tooling; `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/workflows/components.py`
    34	binds handler refs and product policy metadata; and
    35	`arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipeline.py` is the public package facade. This split
    36	is the current problem: the readable Python source exists, but the builder and
    37	component tables still carry a second source of route/policy/handler truth.
    38	
    39	The current graph is a declarative step/route graph:
    40	
    41	- steps are `Step(...)` objects with ids such as `prep`, `plan`, `critique`, `gate`, `revise`, `tiebreaker_run`, `tiebreaker_decide`, `finalize`, `execute`, `review`, `halt`, and `override`;
    42	- each phase step stores a `metadata["handler_ref"]` pointing back to `arnold_pipelines.megaplan.handlers:*`;
    43	- route labels and condition refs represent the coarse graph branches;
    44	- a few policy slots are attached, such as loop metadata on `revise` and `tiebreaker_decide`, suspension routes on `gate` and `review`, and control transition slots on `gate`, `review`, and `tiebreaker_decide`.
    45	
    46	The high-level route comments in `planning.py` show the intended shape:
    47	
    48	```text
    49	prep -> plan -> critique -> gate
    50	                          |-- proceed -> finalize -> execute -> review -> halt
    51	                          |-- iterate -> revise -> critique
    52	                          |-- tiebreaker -> tiebreaker_run -> tiebreaker_decide -> critique
    53	                          |-- escalate / abort / suspend / force-proceed -> override
    54	```
    55	
    56	Relevant source locations:
    57	
    58	| Area | Source |
    59	| --- | --- |
    60	| Product-readable authored workflow | `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/workflows/workflow.py` |
    61	| Package facade | `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/pipeline.py` |
    62	| Workflow builder/lowering adapter | `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/workflows/planning.py` |
    63	| Step/component definitions and handler refs | `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/workflows/components.py` |
    64	| Canonical route/policy canonicalization | `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/workflows/planning.py` |
    65	| Legacy state-machine topology | `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/_core/workflow_data.py:45` |
    66	| Canonical state constants | `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/planning/state.py:7` |
    67	
    68	The separate state-machine data in `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/_core/workflow_data.py` is simpler than the handler behavior. It defines transitions such as:
    69	
    70	- `initialized -> prep -> prepped` and `initialized -> prep -> awaiting_human_verify`;
    71	- `planned -> critique -> critiqued`;
    72	- `critiqued -> gate/revise/tiebreaker/override`;
    73	- `gated -> finalize`;
    74	- `finalized -> execute`;
    75	- `executed -> review -> done`;
    76	- `blocked -> override force-proceed -> finalized`;
    77	- `tiebreaker_pending -> tiebreaker-run -> tiebreaker_ready`;
    78	- `tiebreaker_ready -> tiebreaker-decide -> critiqued`.
    79	
    80	It also has robustness overrides. For example, `bare` allows `planned -> finalize`, and `light` bypasses the full review path by emptying `STATE_EXECUTED` transitions (`arnold_pipelines/megaplan/_core/workflow_data.py:95`).
   180	  -> human/system decision chooses pick, escalate, or replan
   181	  -> pick goes back into revise/critique, escalate waits for human, replan restarts planning
   182	```
   183	
   184	The graph knows about `tiebreaker_run` and `tiebreaker_decide`, but the researcher/challenger split and decision routing are hidden behind handlers.
   185	
   186	### Finalize
   187	
   188	Finalize turns a gated plan into executable tasks, sense checks, watch items, user actions, validation metadata, and baseline/test-selection details. `handle_finalize()` starts at `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/handlers/finalize.py:1661`.
   189	
   190	One topology-relevant branch is error fallback: `FinalizeBaselineSelectionError` can route back to revise (`arnold_pipelines/megaplan/handlers/finalize.py:64`, `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/handlers/finalize.py:1713`).
   191	
   192	### Execute
   193	
   194	Execute runs the finalized task plan. `handle_execute()` starts at `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/handlers/execute.py:134`.
   195	
   196	Execution hides a substantial workflow:
   197	
   198	- asks for destructive/user-approved confirmation in relevant modes;
   199	- chooses batch or auto-loop execution;
   200	- dispatches tasks by model tier and task complexity;
   201	- forces fresh sessions for review rework or blocked retries;
   202	- tracks blocked tasks and quality-gate failures;
   203	- writes stub reviews when review is skipped;
   204	- transitions directly to done or human verification for no-review robustness levels.
   205	
   206	The large auto-execute loop is `handle_execute_auto_loop()` (`arnold_pipelines/megaplan/execute/batch.py:2278`). Single-batch execution starts at `handle_execute_one_batch()` (`arnold_pipelines/megaplan/execute/batch.py:1201`).
   207	
   208	### Review
   209	
   210	Review judges the completed work. `handle_review()` starts at `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/handlers/review.py:1297`.
   211	
   212	Review can:
   213	
   214	- approve the work;
   215	- request rework;
   216	- block;
   217	- route to human verification for deferred human criteria;
   218	- retry review on infrastructure failure;
   219	- run parallel review checks for extreme robustness;
   220	- classify rework as blocking or advisory;
   221	- cap rework cycles and decide between blocked and force-proceed.
   222	
   223	The main outcome state machine is `_resolve_review_outcome()` (`arnold_pipelines/megaplan/handlers/review.py:722`). Parallel review is dispatched through `run_parallel_review(...)` (`arnold_pipelines/megaplan/handlers/review.py:1466`).
   224	
   225	### Override and human gates
   226	
   227	Override is a human/control-plane dispatcher. `_OVERRIDE_ACTIONS` is defined at `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/handlers/override.py:1763`; `handle_override()` starts at `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/handlers/override.py:1780`.
   228	
   229	Override actions include add-note, abort, force-proceed, replan, recover-blocked, resume-clarify, set-robustness, set-profile, set-model, and set-vendor. Some are side effects; others are hard control-flow edges.
   230	

codex
The current process already has strong phase identities: plan, critique, gate, finalize, execute, review are registered as contracts. That makes the right design a small shared schema plus prompt/schema integration at existing phase boundaries, not a new governance subsystem.
