    72	        )
    73	    output_path = _write_finalize_template(plan_dir, state)
    74	
    75	    return textwrap.dedent(
    76	        f"""
    77	        You are DECOMPOSING the approved plan below into an ordered task DAG. The plan has been
    78	        critiqued, gated, and possibly revised. Do NOT re-evaluate strategy or re-litigate
    79	        decisions - gate has settled those.
    80	
    81	        Project directory:
    82	        {project_dir}
    83	
    84	        {intent_brief_reference(state)}
    85	
    86	        Approved plan:
    87	        {latest_plan}
    88	
    89	        Plan metadata:
    90	        {json_dump(latest_meta).strip()}
    91	
    92	        Gate summary:
    93	        {json_dump(gate).strip()}
    94	
    95	        Your output template is at: {output_path}
    96	        Read this file first — it contains the expected JSON structure (tasks, user_actions, sense_checks, watch_items, meta_commentary).
    97	        Fill the JSON structure with your results and write the file back.
    98	        If you cannot use file tools, return the populated JSON structure inline as your response instead.
    99	
   100	        Requirements:
   101	        - Produce structured JSON only.
   102	        - For each `## Step N:` in the plan, emit 1-N tasks.
   103	        - For each task, emit one sense_check.
   104	        - Default `user_actions` to `[]`. Identify a user_action ONLY when the work is genuinely non-mechanical and the executor literally cannot do it (secrets the human alone holds, identity-bound infra access, legal/license signatories, manual UI smoke tests on production). If a check is mechanical, make it a task — not a user_action. See the detailed guidance below.
   105	        - Do not invent tasks that don't trace to a plan step.
   106	        - How batching works at runtime — shape `depends_on` with this in mind, not just literal sequencing. Tasks that share the same `depends_on` set form one batch (capped at 5). Each batch dispatches as a SINGLE LLM conversation to a SINGLE model, picked by the max(complexity) in that batch. So a c=4 audit plus three c=2 tweaks sharing a batch all run in one turn on the c=4 model. Two consequences you control via DAG shape:
   107	          - Routing: every task in a batch runs on the highest-tier task's model — bundle a c=2 task beside a c=4 sibling and it runs on the pricier model.
   108	          - Cognitive load: one conversation holds all of the batch's tasks in working context. The more disparate the tasks, the higher the risk the model loses the thread or claims completion without doing the work. Wide, mixed batches are the dominant quality failure mode.
   109	        - Three principles for shaping batches — judgment, not arithmetic (there is no mechanical split rule):
   110	          1. Isolate heavyweights. A c=4 or c=5 task usually deserves its own batch. Chain lighter tasks through it via `depends_on` rather than placing them as siblings.
   111	          2. Bundle context-related light work. Several c=2/c=3 tasks touching the same files or contracts batch well together.
   112	          3. Never emit more than 5 actionable parallel siblings. If a step legitimately fans out wider, linearize via `depends_on` so the runtime batcher sees at most 5 at a time.
   113	        - Do not include `validation` or `coverage_complete` fields - the harness computes those.
   114	        {final_task_guidance}
   115	        - `tasks` must be an ordered array of task objects. Every task object must include:
   116	          - `id`: short stable task ID like `T1`
   117	          - `description`: concrete work item
   118	          - `depends_on`: array of earlier task IDs or `[]`
   119	          - `status`: always `"pending"` at finalize time
   120	          - `executor_notes`: always `""` at finalize time
   121	          - `reviewer_verdict`: always `""` at finalize time
   122	          - `kind`: indicating the type of work. One of:
   123	            - `code`: writes or modifies source files (executor must produce `files_changed`).
   124	            - `test`: writes or modifies test files, or runs the test suite (executor must produce `files_changed` in tests/ OR `commands_run` containing pytest/test invocations).
   125	            - `audit`: read-only investigation — grep, code review, schema inspection (executor evidence is `executor_notes` describing findings; no `files_changed` expected).
   126	            - `research`: external research or non-code investigation (executor evidence is `executor_notes`).
   127	            - `docs`: writes documentation files (executor must produce `files_changed`).
   128	            If unsure, default to `code`.
   129	          - `complexity`: integer 1–5 complexity score. This score routes the task to a
   130	            model at execution time, so adjudicate it deliberately — do not guess. Use the rubric:
   131	            - 1 = trivial, mechanical, single-file change with no logic to reason about
   132	                  (rename, constant bump, comment, import). A weak model cannot get it wrong.
   133	            - 2 = simple, localized change plus the obvious test update; one file or two,
   134	                  logic is linear and the failure mode is obvious.
   135	            - 3 = multi-file change with non-trivial logic, control flow, or data shape the
   136	                  executor must hold in its head; correctness is not self-evident from the diff.
   137	            - 4 = a wrong implementation would be NON-LOCAL and not caught by an obvious test:
   138	                  the error propagates through a shared contract or invariant that code you did
   139	                  NOT edit relies on. Touching several files is NOT sufficient on its own — a
   140	                  mechanical multi-file change whose failure is local and test-evident is tier 2-3.
   141	            - 5 = fundamental system-invariant change with high regression risk: concurrency that
   142	                  cascades, a schema/wire-format others build on, a state machine, or a security/
   143	                  auth boundary. ONLY when a subtle error would pass the full suite and corrupt
   144	                  state or break a system-wide contract — not merely "important" or central code.
   145	            Score on the hardest REALISTIC aspect of the task, not a worst-case imagining. When
   146	            genuinely torn between two tiers, choose the LOWER one UNLESS you can name the specific
   147	            cascading, test-evading failure that earns the higher tier in the justification.
   148	            You are not the model that executes this task, and the score neither saves nor
   149	            costs you anything — so do not lowball to seem efficient and do not highball to
   150	            play safe. Both distort routing: a too-low score sends a hard task to a model
   151	            that fails it; a too-high score burns a premium model on trivial work. Score
   152	            what the task honestly requires, no higher and no lower.
   153	            FLOOR: a task is NEVER below tier 4 only when it GENUINELY changes the semantics of
   154	            concurrency, a schema/state-machine, a security/auth boundary, or a contract other
   155	            code actually depends on — because a subtle error there passes tests but is wrong.
   156	            This floor does NOT apply to mechanical edits that merely sit near such code, or that
   157	            add a new interface nothing relies on yet; score those on the rubric (usually 2-3).
   158	          - `complexity_justification`: REQUIRED. One or two sentences that argue, specifically,
   159	            why this task sits at exactly that tier — cite the concrete files, interfaces, or
   160	            risk that places it there (e.g. "touches the auth middleware contract used by 4
   161	            call sites, so a mistake is non-local → tier 4"). A bare restatement of the rubric
   162	            ("this is complex") is not acceptable; the justification must be defensible against
   163	            a reviewer who disagrees.
   164	        - `watch_items` must be an array of strings covering runtime risks, critique concerns, and assumptions to keep visible during execution.
   165	        - `sense_checks` must be an array with one verification question per task. Every sense-check object must include:
   166	          - `id`: short stable ID like `SC1`
   167	          - `task_id`: the related task ID
   168	          - `question`: reviewer verification question
   169	          - `verdict`: always `""` at finalize time
   170	        {user_actions_guidance}
   171	        - `meta_commentary` must be a single string with execution guidance, gotchas, or judgment calls that help the executor succeed.
   172	        - Preserve information that strong existing artifacts already capture well: execution ordering, watch-outs, reviewer checkpoints, and practical context.
   173	        - The structured output should be self-contained: an executor reading only `finalize.json` should have everything needed to work.
   174	        - Keep the task count proportional to the work. A simple 1-2 file fix should be 2 tasks: (1) apply the fix, (2) run tests. Do NOT create separate "inspect" or "read" tasks for simple changes — the executor can read and fix in one step. Only create more tasks when the work has genuinely independent stages.
   175	        - One task is the unit of ONE worker turn: the executor must IMPLEMENT it, VERIFY it, and emit its result envelope inside a single ~15-minute conversation. A task that cannot realistically finish in one turn is mis-sized and WILL fail — the worker reports `blocked: "turn ended before implementation"` or runs out the clock mid-edit. Size every task to fit one turn; raising `complexity` does NOT buy more turns, it only routes to a stronger model for the SAME single turn. When a step is a large mechanical refactor, SPLIT it across tasks instead of bundling: "add the new abstraction + its unit tests" (T1) → "migrate consumer A" (T2, depends_on T1) → "migrate consumer B + parity tests" (T3, depends_on T2). A single task that says "extract this 2000-line file into modules and write tests" or "consolidate the roots AND migrate three registries AND add parity tests" is a god-task — decompose it into one task per consumer/module, each independently completable and verifiable.
   176	        - {task_field_guidance}
   177	        """
   178	    ).strip()
   179	
   180	
   181	def _write_finalize_template(
   182	    plan_dir: Path,
   183	    state: PlanState,
   184	) -> Path:
   185	    """Write the finalize output template file and return its path.
   186	
   187	    The template provides the expected top-level keys with empty
   188	    collections so the model only has to populate them.  No ID
   189	    prepopulation is needed — the model generates task and
   190	    sense-check IDs from the approved plan.
   191	    """
   192	    import json
   193	
   194	    template: dict[str, object] = {
   195	        "tasks": [],
   196	        "user_actions": [],
   197	        "sense_checks": [],
   198	        "watch_items": [],
   199	        "meta_commentary": "",
   200	    }
   201	
   202	    output_path = plan_dir / "finalize_output.json"
   203	    output_path.write_text(json.dumps(template, indent=2), encoding="utf-8")
   204	    return output_path

